{"availability":"available","full":"narcisoignacio/so-what","name":"so-what","description":"Explore this public GitHub project.","category":"Other","language":"HTML","stars":0,"forks":0,"created":"2026-09-28T23:38:34Z","updated":"2026-10-11T01:44:09Z","branch":"main","homepage":"https://so-what-six.vercel.app","topics":[],"demo":"https://so-what-six.vercel.app/","color":"#25392f","ink":"#d1e5c5","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-11T06:14:57.874Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-11T06:14:57.874Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-11T06:14:57.874Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-11T06:14:57.874Z","stars":0,"forks":0}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Climate data is hard to connect to everyday life. A county heat projection or a tract-level pollution percentile doesn't tell you what it means for the bus stop where you wait every morning, or whether it's getting worse.","So What? is an interactive map of Los Angeles County that pins everyday places: bus stops, parks, playgrounds and schools. For each place it names the climate-related risks there (air pollution, heat, wildfire and sea level rise) and gives each one a \"So What?\": one plain sentence about what that risk means for a person at that place. For example, at a bus stop in a strong urban heat island: \"This neighborhood traps more heat from pavement and buildings than 94% of LA County neighborhoods. A long summer wait in direct sun can cause heat illness, especially for older riders.\"","The site is my final project for Harvard's CS50x. It's also meant to be a public site for LA County residents. The first goal is that someone who has never been to LA can open one place and understand its risks in about 30 seconds."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/narcisoignacio/so-what/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"360eeb4119cee0ae7c627312d61a8ec33c3bc5a9e323a0126de5e9226c1c8e1b","extractedAt":"2026-10-11T06:14:58.005Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-11T06:14:58.005Z","projectInsights":{"versi